@lib_republic
is-special: true
go-to: menu

= How the Republic governs itself

The **Galactic Senate** is the Republic's legislature. Thousands of delegations represent sectors and member worlds, and meet in the great rotunda on Coruscant. Senators sit in loose **blocs**, not formal parties. Each bloc's share of the Senate changes every year at the **New Senate Session**, as home worlds re-align their delegations.

The Senate elects the **Supreme Chancellor**, who presides over the Senate and leads the government, for a four-year term, and at most two terms. A Chancellor needs a governing **coalition** of blocs holding a majority. The Chancellor can be removed by a **vote of no confidence**.

The coalition shares out the chairs of the great **Senate committees**: Finance & Commerce, Judiciary & Intelligence, Security & Defence, and Outer Rim Affairs. Whoever holds a chair controls that part of the Republic's business. For the Caucus, each chair unlocks new *Senate Affairs* cards.

Since the **Ruusan Reformation** a thousand years ago, the Republic has had no standing army. Its security rests on the **Judicial Forces**, the member worlds' own defence forces and the **Jedi Order**, which serves the Republic but is not governed by the Chancellor. The constitution allows the Senate to grant the Chancellor **emergency powers** in a crisis.

@lib_blocs
is-special: true
go-to: menu

= The blocs of the Senate

**[+ rc_name +]** (us): reformers from Alderaan, Chandrila, Corellia, Rodia and later Naboo. Strongest in the Core and Colonies.

**[+ mod_name +]**: the Core establishment, cautious and comfortable. Valorum's bloc. Weakened by corruption and scandal.

**[+ pal_name +]**: Senator Palpatine of Naboo and his allies, Mas Amedda among them. Small at first. Grows on corruption, fear and the Chancellor's office.

**[+ guild_name +]**: the Trade Federation, Commerce Guild, Banking Clan and Techno Union. Strongest in the corporate holdings. Retaliates with credit strikes when squeezed.

**[+ rim_name +]**: populists from the neglected Mid and Outer Rim. Grows with Rim hardship. A natural ally for Rim reform, and a reservoir for separatism.

**[+ sec_name +]**: Kuat, Anaxes, Eriadu and others who want an army and order. Grows with fear.

**[+ ind_name +]**: unaligned worlds and neutrals, later gathered by Satine of Mandalore. Easily courted.

**[+ cis_name +]**: forms when Dooku's movement begins, from worlds thinking of leaving. Leaves the Senate entirely when war breaks out.

**[+ rfc_name +]**: forms only if the Caucus's Rimward wing splits away.

Every bloc's leader can change as the galaxy changes, and a new leader can change how the bloc votes and whom it will govern with. See *Leaders and their views*.

@lib_regions
is-special: true
go-to: menu

= The regions of the galaxy

Each region sends a share of the Senate's delegations. Each bloc's strength is the sum of its support in every region, weighted by the region's size.

**Core Worlds**: rich, old and powerful. **Colonies & Inner Rim**: prosperous and security-minded. **Mid Rim & Expansion Region**: squeezed between Core and frontier. **Outer Rim Territories**: poor, neglected and preyed upon. **Corporate holdings**: worlds owned outright by the guilds.

When worlds secede, their delegations leave, and the region shrinks. The Senate that remains leans toward the Core, and toward the Chancellor. This is how the canon Senate came to grant Palpatine so much.

@lib_factions
is-special: true
go-to: menu

= The factions of the Ruusan Caucus

**Institutionalists**: proceduralists devoted to the Ruusan constitution. The Caucus's backbone.

**Rimward**: champions of the Outer Rim, of enfranchisement and of abolition. They split away to form the Rim Freedom Caucus if they are ignored for too long.

**Pacifists**: believers in law over arms. They resign their posts if pushed too far toward war.

**Hawks**: defenders of the Republic by force if need be. Distrustful of every Chancellor, they defect to the Security Bloc if the Caucus seems weak.

Each faction has a **strength**, a **dissent** level and a **leader**. Overall dissent reduces almost every gain the Caucus makes. An advisor from a faction keeps its dissent in check. If a faction's dissent reaches about 60 (70 for the Institutionalists), it will break away: sooner under a firm leader, later under a pragmatist. A faction that breaks takes its senators with it. Under a firm leader it founds its own party; otherwise it joins the nearest bloc (Hawks the Security Bloc, Rimward the Rimward Coalition, Pacifists the Independents, Institutionalists the Core Moderates). Each leader has their own demands, shown in the Status panel when they are not met.

@lib_leaders
is-special: true
go-to: menu

= Leaders and their views

Every Senate bloc and every faction of the Caucus has a leader, and leaders change with the times. A neglected Rim turns to radicals. A frightened Senate turns to hardliners. A faction whose demands are met turns to pragmatists. When the galaxy pushes hard enough, a challenger stands. The Caucus can stay out, back a candidate with political capital, or, in another bloc, make a policy offer that helps the softer candidate. Backing the loser has a price.

**What a leader changes**

• *Firmness.* Hardliners make their bloc vote as one, hold grudges, and are hard to court. Pragmatists swing with good relations and forgive quickly. Inside the Caucus, a firm faction leader makes the faction quicker to anger and quicker to break away; a pragmatist makes it patient.

• *Views.* Each leader leans for or against Rim reform, regulating the guilds, emergency powers, the army, Senate oversight, peace talks and no-confidence votes, and their bloc votes accordingly.

• *Partners.* Some leaders refuse to govern alongside certain blocs, which can rule out a coalition entirely.

• *Demands.* Each Caucus faction leader has their own demands, shown in the Status panel when they are not met.

• *The galaxy.* Radical Rim leaders feed separatism, and a Banking Clan hardliner pushes the guilds toward the Confederacy. Pragmatists cool both.

**Who rises, and when**

• *Rimward Coalition.* Ainlee Teem (populist) leads at first. Orn Free Taa (dealmaker) rises when the Rim gets relief and the Senate is corrupt. Tal Ardana (radical) rises when the Rim suffers or its worlds secede. Ardana will not govern with the Core Moderates, the guilds or the Security Bloc, and stokes separatism.

• *Commerce Guilds.* Lott Dod (Trade Federation) leads at first. San Hill (Banking Clan hardliner) rises when the guilds are squeezed or Dooku's movement grows. Rush Clovis (pragmatist) can rise after 25 BBY if the guilds are treated fairly.

• *Core Moderates.* Finis Valorum, then Davan Quill (establishment). Fang Zar (reformist) rises when the Senate grows alarmed at the Chancellor's powers.

• *Security Bloc.* Orvos Hask (law and order) leads at first. Once Wilhuff Tarkin has made his name, he can take over, especially when fear is high or the war goes badly. Giddean Danu (constitutional hawk) rises when the Senate reasserts control of the army.

• *Independents.* Tamsar Vell (broker) leads at first. Satine Kryze (neutralist) speaks for peace in a war-weary galaxy. Corwin Shale (isolationist) rises when money is short.

• *Caucus factions.* Each faction starts under a mainstream leader. Corruption and emergency powers raise a firm Institutionalist, Chi Eekway Papanoida. Rim hardship raises a radical Rimward leader, Jessa Corvane. War-weariness raises an absolutist Pacifist, Tamra Shen. Fear and defeats raise a militarist Hawk, Darra Voss. A faction whose demands are met turns to pragmatists: Terr Taneel, Hollin Rusk, Orrin Dask and Talia Morn.

@lib_meters
is-special: true
go-to: menu

= The state of the Republic, explained

**Political capital** is the Caucus's currency. Patrons contribute each year, and most important actions spend it.

**Faith in the Republic** falls with corruption, fear and emergency powers, and rises with reform. Low faith feeds the Chancellor's bloc.

**Corruption** rises slowly by itself. It feeds Palpatine's appeal as a strong, clean leader, and it feeds separatism.

**Fear** rises with secession and war. It feeds the Security Bloc and the case for emergency powers.

**Outer Rim hardship** drives Rim separatism. Relief, enfranchisement and abolition reduce it. Guild credit strikes and war increase it.

**Separatism** in the Outer Rim, Mid Rim and corporate worlds decides how many worlds leave once Count Dooku's movement begins.

**Emergency powers** measure how much authority the Senate has handed the Chancellor. Each step on the ladder is a Senate vote.

**Evidence** is what the Caucus can prove about the Sith. Below *compelling*, accusations will backfire.

**Senate resolve** is how seriously the Senate takes the danger to its own authority. The Defence Pact, a no-confidence vote and the Petition all need it.

**In the corridors** is the only hint the game gives of the Sith Lord's hidden progress.

For how these numbers feed each other, see *How the systems interact*.

@lib_systems
is-special: true
new-page: true
go-to: lib_sys_menu

= How the systems interact

Every number in the Status panel feeds several others, so a problem left alone in one place spreads to the rest. When you play a card, the turn ends and the galaxy moves in this order:

• The calendar advances, and card and advisor cooldowns tick down.

• Each faction's dissent drifts, and relations with other blocs fade toward their natural level.

• Corruption, fear, faith, Rim hardship, separatism and the Sith's hidden plan update.

• Each bloc's support in each region drifts toward a natural level set by those numbers. Your campaigns push it away from that level for a while.

• Worlds with strong separatism secede. In wartime, the front moves.

• Events fire. Seats are recounted only every January and at Chancellor elections, so the polls run ahead of the seats.

Figures are per quarter. Half-year turns count double.

